WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.342
This is Toft.

2
00:00:01.342 --> 00:00:03.738
Here is what it does, in two minutes.

3
00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.780
With AI, coding is solved.

4
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.804
Your staff and consultants describe what they need, and an agent writes it.

5
00:00:11.804 --> 00:00:16.359
So the question is no longer how to build an app, but where it runs.

6
00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:21.852
Toft is that place: every app, dashboard, report and job, each with a named owner.

7
00:00:23.152 --> 00:00:26.650
Here, Claude Code is asked for a meeting-room booking board.

8
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.965
It writes the app, deploys it to Toft with one command, and tests it live.

9
00:00:30.965 --> 00:00:34.347
A few minutes later, the facilities team is booking rooms.

10
00:00:40.147 --> 00:00:45.074
Which means you no longer need a low-code platform, or a Business Intelligence tool,

11
00:00:45.074 --> 00:00:45.836
to get there.

12
00:00:45.836 --> 00:00:48.827
This dashboard is just an app, written by an agent.

13
00:00:48.827 --> 00:00:53.637
And it runs inside your organization, with access to your data, behind your single

14
00:00:53.637 --> 00:00:54.106
sign-on.

15
00:00:55.406 --> 00:01:00.983
And the apps already scattered around your organization, on old servers, desktops

16
00:01:00.983 --> 00:01:06.698
and dashboard boxes, can be brought under the same umbrella, and modernized as they

17
00:01:06.698 --> 00:01:07.042
move.

18
00:01:07.042 --> 00:01:11.999
Shiny, Streamlit, R Markdown, plain scripts: one home, one set of rules.

19
00:01:13.299 --> 00:01:15.318
Shipping one is a single step.

20
00:01:15.318 --> 00:01:19.492
Point Toft at a Git repository, or push from the command line.

21
00:01:19.492 --> 00:01:25.079
Toft detects Python, R, Node or Go, builds it, checks its dependencies against your

22
00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:28.175
policies, and puts it live at its own address.

23
00:01:29.475 --> 00:01:34.341
This one was stopped before it went live: it carried a package with a critical

24
00:01:34.341 --> 00:01:35.214
vulnerability.

25
00:01:35.214 --> 00:01:37.459
A bad build never takes an app down.

26
00:01:37.459 --> 00:01:41.077
Every release is kept, and any version can be rolled back.

27
00:01:42.377 --> 00:01:46.491
Owners decide who sees each app, person by person or by group.

28
00:01:46.491 --> 00:01:50.805
Secrets are encrypted, and every app runs isolated from the rest.

29
00:01:52.105 --> 00:01:57.417
Scheduled jobs and reports run here too, with their history, and a message when one

30
00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:57.801
fails.

31
00:01:59.101 --> 00:02:00.557
And IT keeps control.

32
00:02:00.557 --> 00:02:06.033
One place to see every app, who owns it, what it uses, and every change, in the

33
00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:06.726
audit log.

34
00:02:08.026 --> 00:02:09.777
Let your people build.

35
00:02:09.777 --> 00:02:11.290
Keep IT in control.

36
00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.519
Get Toft at gettoft dot com.

